## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical E2E testing with per-user feature flags, data isolation, and telemetry verification in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Feature flags per-user, test data isolation, mocking services
- End-to-end UI verification and telemetry validation
- Flakiness prevention in a shared staging environment

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright example (high level)
const flagApi = new MockFlagService({ userA: true, userB: false });

test('enabled path renders tour', async ({ page }) => {
  await loginAs('userA');
  await flagApi.setUserFlag('userA', true);
  await page.goto('/onboarding');
  await expect(page.locator('.tour-step')).toBeVisible();
  // complete tour steps
});

test('disabled path skips tour', async ({ page }) => {
  await loginAs('userB');
  await flagApi.setUserFlag('userB', false);
  await page.goto('/onboarding');
  await expect(page.locator('.tour-step')).toHaveCount(0);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this for multiple flags and regions?
- What strategies ensure telemetry data remains accurate across retries?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for offline-first applications with service workers and cross-tab interactions are common in modern web development. This question evaluates practical E2E thinking around offline caching, network replay, idempotent API design, and conflict resolution at a beginner level.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first UX and service workers
- Local storage and IndexedDB persistence
- Idempotent server updates with PUT
- Cross-tab concurrency and conflict handling
- Deterministic data seeds for repeatable tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright E2E test for offline-first note app
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test.describe('Offline-first note app', () => {
  test('should create note offline, sync when online, and handle idempotency', async ({ page }) => {
    // Set up deterministic seed for reproducible data
    const noteId = 'test-note-001';
    const noteContent = 'Test note content';

    // Simulate offline by intercepting network requests
    await page.route('**/api/**', (route) => route.abort('failed'));

    // Create note while offline
    await page.goto('/app');
    await page.fill('[data-testid="note-editor"]', noteContent);
    await page.click('[data-testid="save-button"]');

    // Verify note is cached locally
    const localNote = await page.evaluate(() => localStorage.getItem('draft'));
    expect(localNote).toContain(noteContent);

    // Restore network and mock PUT endpoint
    await page.unroute('**/api/**');
    await page.route('**/api/notes/**', (route) => {
      route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ revision: 'rev-123' }) });
    });

    // Trigger sync
    await page.click('[data-testid="sync-button"]');
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="sync-status"]')).toHaveText('Synced');

    // Verify idempotent behavior - re-submit should not create duplicate
    const initialRequestCount = 1;
    await page.click('[data-testid="sync-button"]');
    // Assert no duplicate entries on server
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests at scale must validate multi-tenant data isolation, region-resident content, and flag-driven UX under outages. This probes orchestration, data management, and resilience.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenancy isolation and data residency
- Content personalization pipelines and identity graphs
- Feature flags and A/B test routing
- Regional outages and retry strategies
- Test data seeding and reproducibility

## Code Example
```javascript
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('e2e: multi-tenant personalization with feature flags', async ({ page }) => {
  const tenant = 'tenantA';
  await page.route('https://content.example.com/api/personalize', route =>
    route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ banner: 'A', content: 'X' }) })
  );
  await page.goto(`https://content.example.com/${tenant}?region=eu`);
  await expect(page.locator('#banner')).toHaveText('A');
});
```

